00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when I was nine years old I asked my  parents for an allowance since I had  been diligently feeding the cats they  agreed and we settled on three dollars a  week payable on Fridays the first Friday  rolls around and I am so excited we're  sitting at the dinner table and my dad  slowly pulls out a crisp $2 bill and  then a stack of four quarters then he  takes out these two plastic yogurt cups  with Sharpie on them one of them says  family the other one charity dad what's  with the cups my parents calmly  explained to me that out of every  paycheck come certain contributions and  that from my three dollar allowance  twenty-five cents was going to a Family  Tax and twenty-five cents was going to  charity  Oh nine-year-old Alexis with pissed I  done all of the calculations and it was  now going to take literally forever for  me to buy my summer pass the amusement  park so as you can imagine this was my  first taste of the frustrations of  personal finance as we all know this  really pales in comparison to what a lot  of people face on this front people rank  money related concerns as worse than  death and just behind being alone for  the rest of your life and maybe it's no  wonder when we look at it and we find  out that almost half of Americans don't  have four hundred dollars in the case of  an emergency and some people really  can't make this math work  but I know a lot of highly educated  highly motivated people with resources  who still seem to make irrational  decisions about their money I spent the  past seven years working with people who  know a lot about how you and I manage  our finances I was at the Consumer  Financial Protection Bureau in DC doing  strategy consulting for big retail banks  and most recently at a consumer FinTech  and even in these environments I came  across people who made irrational  decisions one day I asked my teammate  what about his money habits he's an  expert in helping banks manage risk and  plan for the long term he let me know  all his savings in a checking account  earning zero interest there's got to be  something deeper going on here the  solution I argue is to start from  psychology not from spreadsheets picking  a psychology first approach to personal  finance gives me hope that we can each  learn to better manage our own money and  also change the way that we as a society  talk about personal finance so how can  we make this happen I've come up with  three steps that I'm going to share with  you today so step one understand your  personal financial psychology a few  examples of what this could look like as  Millennials we're investing a lot less  in the stock market than previous  generations and experts say we may have  developed risk aversion from growing up  during the recession people from poorer  households may have a scarcity mindset  which can come from having grown up in  an environment where money is uncertain  and so you end up spending more than you  should be saving  personally I come from a house that kept  spending and personal finance top of  mind as you may have gotten from that  yogurt cup day so this unfortunately can  have its own ramifications and two  weekends ago I was up in San Francisco  and I walked half a mile in five-inch  heels instead of paying five dollars for  parking this is something I'm working on  so where are you coming from and what  does that lead you to do today debt to  implement a system to counteract those  tendencies here at the GSB we talk a lot  about culture building about setting up  incentives for our teams I'm saying we  should do the same for ourselves and  there's a lot of great new innovative  tools out there that can help with us  but they only help when you start from  where you are and build a system around  that so for example if it's risk  aversion you can outsource your  investing activity to a robo advisor who  can take that accountability off of your  plate and make you feel more comfortable  if the issue is a scarcity mindset  instead you can use an app that will  help you with different saving activity  like rounding up your purchases to the  nearest dollar and setting aside that  extra change into a savings account one  thing we can all benefit from it's  finding ways to associate our financial  chores with positive experiences and  with rewards so every month I get a  calendar alert that says adventure fund  and there's just a bunch of exclamation  points over here and like maybe a few  emojis and this is my alert to move my  extra cash for my checking account into  a high interest savings account so I can  earn a few extra points of interest on  money that would be sitting around  anyway by finding a way to take this  financial chore and associate it with  something I love with travel with the  feelings I'm going to  now when I actually get to use that  savings I get to move that future  feeling of reward to the present this  sims are the ways that we can actually  build habits  it's the ways that we can end up  sticking to our goals and through  automation and association with positive  feelings and rewards we can actually end  up changing our behaviors which leads me  to step 3 you have this system now what  share it a whopping 43% of Americans  don't know how much money their spouse  makes think about that how are we  supposed to plan for shared futures when  we don't have this information how are  our loved ones supposed to hold us  accountable to our goals how can we help  the people we care about in our lives  we're taking classes right now on  accounting and finance we're making  these huge decisions about taking on  debt about what career we're gonna have  what implication that's gonna have for  our salaries and yet none of us are  talking about money even if you're in a  good place with us I guarantee you  someone close to you is sitting here  with a really bad hit in their stomach  and we all have a responsibility to  change the ways that we talk about money  to take away the shame from that  conversation so if you've recently gone  through a salary negotiation you can  help someone else get a raise if you've  got a great system for your savings you  can help someone else get over that  hurdle if you just start talking about  money you can welcome someone else who  needs help to do the same  tackling these three steps understand  your personal financial psychology  implement a system to counteract those  tendencies and share it will empower  each of us to live better financial  lives and change the ways that we as a  society approach money I care way too  much about my friends and family to let  them struggle unnecessarily with their  finances  you can bet someday I'm gonna tax my own  kids allowance and by that point it'll  probably be more like a dollar instead  of 25 cents they'll pay it forward with  a little less than about inflation  [Applause]  [Music]

